{
  "chapter": "observability/what-a-trace-must-answer",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "Evals tell you a run was wrong; traces tell you why. APM can show every service behaving",
    "normally while the agent made the wrong decision inside a technically healthy request.",
    "Eight questions a trace must answer alone, and the expensive one is what was in the",
    "window: large, different every turn, and the only one that explains the decision.",
    "Split the storage. Metadata and a content hash go to the observability backend and full",
    "payloads to cheap object storage; the hash is both the join key and an integrity check.",
    "Sample the boring and keep the interesting: everything escalated, errored, blocked or",
    "slow, and a small draw of the rest.",
    "Use the business entity as the correlation id and the trace, the event history, the eval",
    "score, the audit record and the ticket all join without a mapping table.",
    "Record a truncation boundary. Without it, an answer that failed because a field was cut",
    "is indistinguishable from one that failed because the model ignored it \u2014 the difference",
    "between fixing a tool and rewriting a prompt."
  ],
  "policy": {
    "questions": [
      "promptId",
      "modelVersion",
      "windowContents",
      "toolCalls",
      "toolResults",
      "statedReasoning",
      "stepCost",
      "divergedAt"
    ],
    "alwaysKeep": [
      "escalated",
      "error",
      "blocked"
    ],
    "sampleBps": 500,
    "outlierLatencyMs": 30000,
    "maxBackendBytes": 1000
  },
  "cases": [
